WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.560
Vsauce! Kevin here.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:09.520
You may know May 10th, 2020 as Children’s Day&nbsp;
in the Maldives or Mother’s Day in Mexico. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:15.360
Bono’s 60th birthday, or the anniversary&nbsp;
of serial killer John Wayne Gacy’s death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.520
But on May 10th, 2020, Australia had a&nbsp;
very serious question -- as a nation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.000
it collectively needed to know&nbsp;
how many sides a circle has.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.000
The answer is... a little more nuanced than&nbsp;
it may seem. There’s the easy math answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.200
the real life answer, and the answer&nbsp;
that’s part hard math and part real life.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.280
Listen, the answer can be pretty straightforward.&nbsp;
If you’re LAZY, or if you want to stick to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:52.080
most basic math. A polygon is a two-dimensional&nbsp;
shape on a plane that has a defined number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:59.680
sides -- or actually, angles. It’s right&nbsp;
in the name, the Greek “poluganon” means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:08.320
“many-angled.” Triangle is a trigon, three&nbsp;
angles. Tridecagon, 13 angles. And 13 sides.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:19.200
But how many angles does a circle have? Well…&nbsp;
none, really. Is that a… none-agon? Naught-agon?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:27.120
Zilch-agon? There aren’t any points at which&nbsp;
two lines make a vertex. That can make it a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:35.360
monogon, which Euclidean geometry categorizes as a&nbsp;
degenerate polygon because its endpoints overlap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:42.720
But if you’re just looking at a circle, you aren’t&nbsp;
seeing any angles -- you’re really just seeing one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:51.360
continuous side. There’s the first answer, a&nbsp;
circle has one side. The end? Right? WRONG!

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.360
WHAT IS A SIDE? This is where it gets tricky.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:03.440
In math, a side is really an edge or a face&nbsp;
depending on whether we’re working in 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:12.720
or 3 dimensions. But in real life, a side is… a&nbsp;
side. You refer to the left side of your body or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:20.320
the side of a ship, or the outside and inside of&nbsp;
your house. “Side” in Olde English pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:27.200
set the tone for how we think of the word:&nbsp;
it meant the left or right flank of a person.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:33.040
But when we think about what a side&nbsp;
is, it’s always relative to the whole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:38.960
Humans have bilateral symmetry, so the left&nbsp;
of your body is a mirror image of the right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:46.240
for the most part. That whole unit -- your body --&nbsp;
means we can break it down into relative sides and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:53.680
have it make sense. A side is about position&nbsp;
relative to something greater. Like, a side dish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:03:04.080
of your meal is relative to the main dish of the&nbsp;
meal. The steak or whatever. Spaghetti? Hot Dogs?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.680
You know what I mean! You can’t have the port&nbsp;
side of a ship without drawing a mental line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:16.000
of symmetry lengthwise in its center. You&nbsp;
can’t talk about how the inside of your thigh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.320
hurts without silently differentiating&nbsp;
it from the obvious outside of your leg.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.720
Or let’s talk about starfish!&nbsp;
Look at a starfish because that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:33.360
radial symmetry coming from the center outward.&nbsp;
It’s got 10 faces with two on each arm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:39.840
but we’d say it has five sides -- and each of&nbsp;
those sides has a left side and a right side. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:45.680
it’s got 5 sides or 10 sides depending&nbsp;
on how we’re breaking it down.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.160
It’s positional. It’s&nbsp;
directional. It’s relative. But...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.200
we’re supposed to be talking about&nbsp;
circles! Circles! Okay, Alright, alright.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.000
Well, now we’ve got at least one&nbsp;
more answer -- a circle’s got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.600
an inside and an outside, 2&nbsp;
totally legitimate sides. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.680
that’s probably wrong. Because...
WHAT ABOUT WHEELS? WHEELS.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:16.320
You’ve got a circle and it’s rolling on a&nbsp;
flat surface. Between gravity and friction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.640
eventually it stops. Let’s imagine a wheel&nbsp;
as a perfect circle. Because it’s a perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:28.160
circle and the surface is perfectly flat, the&nbsp;
actual point of contact between the two is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:37.440
infinitesimal in size. Does that make the point&nbsp;
of contact a side? Probably -- and how many are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.240
there? I could roll a circle 10,000 times&nbsp;
and it’s never gonna stop in exactly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.480
same place. That suggests there&nbsp;
are infinite sides -- just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:55.600
the coast of the continent that feverishly&nbsp;
wished for an answer to this very question.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:02.480
The coast of Australia is infinitely&nbsp;
long. Don’t believe me? You should!

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:09.600
Imagine measuring the coastline of Australia&nbsp;
using a measuring stick 20 kilometers long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.320
Then you go back and measure it with a 1 kilometer&nbsp;
measuring stick -- the measurement would actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:20.800
be significantly longer because you’re able to&nbsp;
capture more detail in the twists and turns of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:27.600
coastline. If you did it by the meter? Even more.&nbsp;
Centimeter, Millimeter, nanometer, femtometer… the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:32.640
smaller your unit of measurement,&nbsp;
the more you reduce the uncertainty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:38.320
and increase that coastline. And if&nbsp;
your unit of measurement can become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:46.960
infinitely small, then how does the&nbsp;
coastline NOT become infinitely large?!

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:53.200
It’s the same exact concept with ‘sides’&nbsp;
of a circle. We’ve got an infinite number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:58.080
of points at which a circle can come&nbsp;
to rest as it’s rolling on a plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:03.520
and the only limitation we have is&nbsp;
in our ability to measure it. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:11.440
how many sides would it have if we could count&nbsp;
the atoms, or the electrons, or the quarks?

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.120
Sorry, Australia, but how many&nbsp;
sides does a circle have just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:25.360
doesn’t have a definitive answer -- depending on&nbsp;
how you look at the problem, a circle can have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:31.360
no sides, one side, two sides, or… infinite sides.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:39.040
So what side are YOU on? How many&nbsp;
sides do you think a circle has?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:45.280
Let me know down BELOW in the&nbsp;
bottom side of this video.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:47.840
And as always -- thanks for watching.

